<p>
  The game's handbook groups items and blocks that differ only in a variant into one cycling icon, through
  <code>attributes.handbook.groupBy</code> on their type: a list of code wildcards whose <code>{placeholder}</code>s are filled
  from the variant's own values. <code>armor-{bodypart}-{construction}-*</code> on <code>armor-body-lamellar-iron</code>
  becomes <code>armor-body-lamellar-*</code>, so that groups one armor piece across materials, not all armor.
</p>
<p>
  <code>apps/vs-wiki/src/ingest/grouping.ts</code> reads every item and block type's <code>groupBy</code>, fills the
  placeholders per variant and groups the variants that end up with the same first pattern (the game keys on the first one too).
  A family needs at least two entries in <a href="{{ ('/' ~ lang ~ '/') | url }}items/">Items &amp; Blocks</a>. The example
  shown first is the variant the type's <code>creativeinventory</code> lists, otherwise the first by code.
</p>
<p>
  The game only applies this grouping inside its reference lists (drops, "found in", host rock); its main handbook list still
  shows every variant. The wiki follows that: <a href="{{ ('/' ~ lang ~ '/') | url }}items/">Items &amp; Blocks</a> lists every
  variant, and this page is the separate view of the families. Some families are broader than you might expect (every metal's
  ingot, every rock's slab) because that is what the game's own rule says.
</p>
